{
  "text": "The Guardian article by Kucirkova discusses how the rhythm of children's media, such as fast-paced TV shows, can capture attention but leave little space for reflection and emotional connection. In contrast, slower media allows for more coherent narratives and pauses, which can support prediction, emotion, and memory. However, the study does not show that all fast-paced media harms children or that slower media always produces better learning. Meanwhile, the scholarly study by Jinggangshan University researchers found that AI-assisted writing showed the highest observed writing quality when open collaboration was present, but did not lead to the strongest independent performance. The study's limitations include the six-class allocation, which may have confounded condition with class, timetable, and cohort. Additionally, Week 8 measured near transfer rather than delayed or cross-genre transfer, and the offloading framework requires independent validation.",
